# Keyword-routing table

The keyword-routing table is the **mechanical** part of the routing system. It maps title/body keywords to boards. The operator reads the table to find a board, not to find a keyword.

| Title/body keywords | Board |
|---|---|
| [keyword1, keyword2, ...] | [board-name-1] |
| [keyword3, keyword4, ...] | [board-name-2] |
| ... | ... |

**Notes:**
- [Any specific patterns the operator uses, e.g., "Multi-keyword patterns: 'loop AND requirements' routes to Board A; 'loop AND build' routes to Board B"]
- [Any exceptions or edge cases]
- [When the table was last reviewed]

---

# Cross-board move procedure

The cross-board move canonical pattern:

1. **Close the source ticket** with `--result` citing the superseding ticket id and the reason. The closure is auditable.
2. **Switch active board** to the target board (if your tool has an "active board" concept).
3. **Re-file on the target board** with the full body preserved. The new ticket has a new id; the old ticket is closed.
4. **Restore previous active board** (if step 2 changed it).

**Audit-line on every cross-board move:**

```
## [ISO8601-UTC] cross-board-move — <source_tid> -> <target_board>: <reason>
```

---

# When to add a new board

Add a new board when **at least 2** of the following are true:

1. The work-type has its own worker profile.
2. The work-type has its own cron cadence.
3. The work-type has an operator gate.
4. The work-type has a separate dashboard view.
5. The work-type is expected to have >50 tickets over its lifetime.

If only 1 is true, **don't add a new board.** Use the default board.

---

# When to revise the catalog

The catalog is **not static.** Revise when:

1. A new work type emerges (5+ tickets in a week that don't fit any existing board) → add a new board
2. A board is underused (<5 tickets in a month) → demote to default board
3. A keyword is producing false positives → refine the keyword
4. A cross-board move becomes routine (>5 moves/week) → consider merging or splitting

---

# How the friend uses this template

1. **List the boards you have.** (The friend has 4-8 boards depending on their context.)
2. **Fill in the catalog** for each board, using the 9-section format above.
3. **Design the keyword-routing table** based on the catalog entries. Each board gets a row.
4. **Document the cross-board contracts** if any.
5. **Review the catalog** when the routing system fails (false positives, missed tickets, etc.).
6. **Update the catalog** when the routing system is revised.

The catalog is **operator-context.** The friend running Alice's methodology designs their own catalog for their own context. They do NOT copy the operator's specific boards; they apply the method to derive their own.

---

# Approval

- [ ] Each board has a 9-section entry (domain, use-for, do-not-use-for, routing invariant, workers, cron, operator-gate, cross-board-contract, ...)
- [ ] The keyword-routing table is in operator-context
- [ ] The cross-board move procedure is documented with audit-line format
- [ ] The "when to add a new board" test (2-of-5) is in the catalog
- [ ] The "when to revise" criteria are in the catalog
- [ ] The default board is documented (catch-all, not a junk drawer)

Once all 6 boxes are checked, the catalog is ready.
